        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="editorial-chapter">
        <h2>House Rent Allowance (HRA) Classification in Madhya Pradesh</h2>
        <p>
          In Madhya Pradesh, House Rent Allowance is categorized based on population metrics and municipal corporation limits under state financial rules:
        </p>

        <div class="city-classification-grid">
          <div class="city-box tier-y">
            <h4>Class Y Cities (Major Municipal Corporations)</h4>
            <div class="badge-tag">Five Major Metropolitan Hubs</div>
            <p><strong>Baseline HRA Rate:</strong> 16% to 18% of Basic Pay.</p>
            <p><strong>Covered Cities:</strong> Bhopal Municipal Corporation, Indore Municipal Corporation, Jabalpur, Gwalior, and Ujjain.</p>
            <span class="card-footer-note">Reflects prominent commercial, industrial, and administrative hubs.</span>
          </div>

          <div class="city-box tier-z">
            <h4>Class Z Areas (Other Districts &amp; Rural Stations)</h4>
            <div class="badge-tag">Remaining District Headquarters &amp; Tehsils</div>
            <p><strong>Baseline HRA Rate:</strong> 8% to 9% of Basic Pay.</p>
            <p><strong>Covered Areas:</strong> Sagar, Rewa, Satna, Ratlam, Dewas, Shivpuri, Chhindwara, rural school posts, and gram panchayats.</p>
            <span class="card-footer-note">Baseline rate providing housing subsidies across rural field postings.</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="editorial-chapter">
        <h2>Career Advancement Schemes: Time-Scale Pay (Samayman Vetanman)</h2>
        <p>
          To protect state employees from career stagnation where promotional channels are restricted, the Government of Madhya Pradesh enforces the <strong>Time-Scale Pay Scheme (Samayman Vetanman Niyam)</strong>:
        </p>
        <div class="callout-box">
          <h4>The 10, 20, and 30 Year Upgradation Rule:</h4>
          <p>
            Under the state's Time-Scale Pay guidelines, employees who complete <strong>10, 20, and 30 years</strong> of continuous regular service in a single post without departmental promotion are granted financial upgradation to the next higher pay level:
          </p>
          <ul class="bullet-feature-list">
            <li><strong>First Time-Scale (Pratham Samayman - 10 Years):</strong> Advances the employee to the next promotional grade pay / level in the matrix.</li>
            <li><strong>Second Time-Scale (Dwitiya Samayman - 20 Years):</strong> Grants financial advancement to the second promotional level upon completing 20 years of service.</li>
            <li><strong>Third Time-Scale (Tritiya Samayman - 30 Years):</strong> Grants advancement to the third promotional level upon completing 30 years.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Pay fixation under Samayman Vetanman awards one vertical 3% increment in the existing pay level before placing the employee in the matching or immediate next higher cell of the upgraded level.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="editorial-chapter">
        <h2>Pension Architecture: NPS vs OPS Debate &amp; MP Retirees</h2>
        <p>
          Retirement compensation in Madhya Pradesh involves active policy considerations:
        </p>
        <ul class="bullet-feature-list">
          <li><strong>The Contributory Framework (NPS):</strong> State employees joining regular public service after January 1, 2005 are enrolled under the National Pension System (NPS), contributing 10% of their Basic Pay &plus; DA, with matching state contributions. Employee federations continue active campaigns advocating for the restoration of the defined-benefit <strong>Old Pension Scheme (OPS)</strong> or the state adoption of the <strong>Unified Pension Scheme (UPS)</strong> model.</li>
          <li><strong>State Pensioner Revisions:</strong> Over 4.5 lakh retired state civil servants and family pensioners will see their baseline monthly pension multiplied by the approved 8th CPC fitment factor, elevating the statutory minimum state pension to over <strong>₹20,520 per month</strong> under the 2.28 factor.</li>
          <li><strong>Medical Reimbursement &amp; Health Facilities:</strong> State employees and pensioners benefit from state medical reimbursement rules and health schemes covering empaneled private and government hospitals across Madhya Pradesh.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="editorial-chapter">
        <h2>Digital Payroll Integration via the IFMS MP Portal</h2>
        <p>
          The Government of Madhya Pradesh administers all public wage disbursements through its robust digital financial technology network:
        </p>
        <p>
          The <strong>Integrated Financial Management Information System (IFMS MP - mptreasury.gov.in)</strong> and the Cyber Treasury automate electronic monthly salary processing, e-Salary slips, annual 3% vertical increment credits, Samayman Vetanman upgradations, and GPF/NPS accounting across all 55 districts.
        </p>
        <p>
          When the 8th Pay Commission Government Orders are officially gazetted by the Finance Department (<em>Vitta Vibhag</em>), revised pay matrices and retrospective arrear calculations will be automated within the electronic IFMS network, ensuring direct-benefit electronic bank transfers for all government servants across Madhya Pradesh.
        </p>
      </section>

